=== FAULT M-ALIAS-01: stale model mapping (LATEST_CLAUDE["anthropic/claude-sonnet"] left pointing at an old concrete id) ===
Target: src/chuzom/model_aliases.py, LATEST_CLAUDE dict (single source of truth for `family:latest` alias resolution, per its own module docstring: "update only this when a newer version ships").
Mutation: changed
    "anthropic/claude-sonnet": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-6",
to
    "anthropic/claude-sonnet": "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-5",  # MUTATED: stale mapping, forgot to bump on release
i.e. simulated the literal failure mode the module's own docstring warns about: a maintainer ships a new Sonnet version but forgets to bump this one line, so `family:latest` silently keeps resolving to the PREVIOUS version indefinitely. Same family prefix (anthropic/claude-sonnet), so model_family()/model_matches()/MODELS_PER_PROFILE (all version-agnostic, family-prefix-based) cannot distinguish stale-but-valid from current — by design, for forward compatibility, they also can't detect backward staleness.

Command (targeted):
  cd mutation-lab && .venv-audit/bin/python -m pytest -q -o addopts="" \
    tests/test_model_aliases.py tests/test_calibration.py tests/test_benchmark_registry.py \
    tests/test_benchmark_cat_g.py tests/test_cost.py tests/test_profiles.py
Result: 113 passed. NOT CAUGHT.

Command (full suite, confirmatory):
  cd mutation-lab && .venv-audit/bin/python -m pytest -q --ignore=tests/test_agno_integration.py --timeout=30
Result: ran to 100% completion, "Scenario report written ... 24 scenarios, 24 passed", zero FAILED/ERROR lines, warnings-only tail identical in fingerprint to every known-clean baseline run of this suite. NOT CAUGHT (confirms the targeted-subset result; whole suite, not just the 6 files above, is blind to this fault).

Root cause / why existing tests missed it:
  tests/test_model_aliases.py is the ONLY test file that imports model_aliases directly, and every assertion in it that touches LATEST_CLAUDE compares against `ma.LATEST_CLAUDE[...]` ITSELF (self-referential), never against a hardcoded/independent "this is what the current model actually is" literal:
    - test_resolve_alias_known_and_unknown:  ma.resolve_model_alias(...) == ma.LATEST_CLAUDE["anthropic/claude-opus"]
    - test_chains_resolve_aliases_in_routing_table:  chain[0] == ma.LATEST_CLAUDE["anthropic/claude-opus"]
    - test_opus5_simulation_one_line_bump: uses monkeypatch to inject a NEW value and asserts the resolver follows it — proves the *mechanism* works, says nothing about whether the *current committed value* is actually current.
  This is a textbook "test imports/reads the same source of truth it's supposed to be checking" pathology (mandate item 4): the dict can be wrong in any way and every assertion that dereferences it will still trivially agree with itself. No test anywhere in the ~101k-line suite pins the literal expected-current model id (e.g. asserts LATEST_CLAUDE["anthropic/claude-sonnet"] == "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-6" against an independent expectation, or cross-checks it against a release/changelog manifest, a benchmarks registry "supported models" list, or a "models known as of vX.Y" fixture).
  Downstream guards are structurally blind too: MODELS_PER_PROFILE (profiles.py) and _validate_chain_invariants() only assert FAMILY-prefix membership (deliberately version-agnostic, "so a new version is governed automatically without editing this file") — the exact design choice that makes forward-compat easy is what makes backward staleness undetectable by the same mechanism.
  Also noted in passing: src/chuzom/profiles.py:33-36 (_CLAUDE_CHEAP_MODELS frozenset) hardcodes "anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-6" and "anthropic/claude-haiku-4-5-20251001" as a SEPARATE, independent literal that does NOT derive from LATEST_CLAUDE at all — a second, un-synchronized source of truth for "what is the current Claude model," which this mutation does not even touch and which could drift from LATEST_CLAUDE in the opposite direction with zero cross-check either way. Flagged as an adjacent architectural risk (multiple un-synchronized sources of truth for "current model"), not folded into this fault's verdict.

Impact if real: silent, permanent, and invisible via any test in the suite. A `family:latest` alias resolution would keep every "Sonnet latest" routing decision pinned to a deprecated/retired model indefinitely after a real version bump — degrading quality/cost/capability for every caller using the alias, with CI staying fully green forever, until a human happens to notice by inspection.

Revert: cp /tmp/model_aliases.py.bak back over src/chuzom/model_aliases.py; grep -c MUTATED -> 0 (clean).

