=== FAULT M-HIST-01: fabricated historical row (phantom day injected into query_daily) ===
Target: src/chuzom/dashboard_data.py, query_daily() (line ~260-366).
Mutation: immediately before the function's return list-comprehension, unconditionally
injected daily["1970-01-01"] = {"calls": 999, "tokens": 50000, "saved": 25.0, "tokens_saved": 500}
into the `daily` dict that the return statement iterates over — simulating a real-world bug
class (a leftover demo/seed row, or a "never show an empty chart to a new user" shortcut that
silently fabricates a day of activity not backed by any DB row). All real SQL aggregation logic
was left untouched.

Command:
  "$AUDIT_PY" -m pytest -q -o addopts="" tests/test_dashboard_data.py
Result: 1 failed, 6 passed — CAUGHT.
  FAILED tests/test_dashboard_data.py::test_query_daily_sum_matches_query_window_14d
    AssertionError: daily total calls 1003 != window 14d calls 4 (assert 1003 == 4)
  This test independently re-derives the same 14-day window total via query_window("14d")
  (a SEPARATE code path with its own SQL aggregation, not a call to query_daily) and asserts
  the two reconcile — the fabricated row's 999 phantom calls broke that cross-check immediately.

Notable NON-catch, and why (test-architecture finding worth flagging in the report):
  tests/test_dashboard_data.py::test_session_end_daily_matches_query_daily did NOT fail, despite
  asserting equality between "legacy" _query_daily_14d() (src/chuzom/hooks/session-end.py:1087)
  and "canonical" query_daily(14) (the mutated function). Root cause: _query_daily_14d is a thin
  wrapper that DELEGATES directly to chuzom.dashboard_data.query_daily (see session-end.py:1098-
  1100, explicit v10.1.6 comment: "delegates to chuzom.dashboard_data.query_daily ... so any
  future schema addition only requires updating that module"). Both "consumers" therefore call
  the exact same mutated function and trivially agree with each other on the fabricated row too.
  This test provides ZERO independent verification of query_daily's correctness — it only proves
  two callers of one shared implementation haven't diverged from EACH OTHER, not that either is
  right. It is architecturally similar to the M-ALIAS-01 pathology (a test whose oracle shares
  the same source of truth as the code under test) even though this consolidation was an
  intentional, well-documented refactor (reducing duplicate SQL, which is a legitimate goal) —
  the byproduct is that the docstring's claim "Cross-consumer agreement ... A future regression
  in either consumer would break this" is only true for regressions that affect the two
  call-sites DIFFERENTLY; a regression in the single shared implementation they both now call
  is invisible to this specific test, full stop, regardless of magnitude.

Verdict: CAUGHT — genuinely, and via a real independent cross-check (query_window uses separate
SQL). This is a clean positive control: a fabricated data row is a highly visible, high-magnitude
fault and the totals-reconciliation invariant test built for a different bug class (v9.3 schema
drift) catches it as a side effect. But the sibling "cross-consumer agreement" test in the same
file is a false sense of security for THIS specific fault class post-v10.1.6-consolidation — it
would not catch a fabrication (or any other bug) introduced directly into query_daily's shared
implementation, only a divergence between the two thin call sites, which is now structurally
almost impossible to have.

Reverted: cp /tmp/dashboard_data.py.bak back over dashboard_data.py; grep -c MUTATED -> 0 (clean).
